{"items":[{"index":49,"prompt":"We’re launching a compact basketball ball-handling kit for teens and adult recreational players, and I’d like its front-of-package artwork to make the contents feel connected through movement. On the 1920×1200 canvas, center a basketball at (960, 610), about 225 pixels in radius. Arrange exactly five satellite pieces of equipment around it: one training cone, one hand pump, one stopwatch, one whistle, and a pair of wristbands. Keep each silhouette distinct and fully visible, positioned at separated points along two looping paths; don’t let the gear overlap one another.

Build the paths as ellipses centered on the ball: an upright ellipse with radii 360 and 190 pixels, and a second ellipse with radii 650 and 130 pixels, rotated 30 degrees clockwise. Where the loops cross, weave them consistently: the outer loop passes in front at the upper-left and lower-right crossings and behind at the other two. The basketball should cover the portions of the smaller loop that fall inside its silhouette. Draw both paths behind the satellite equipment, breaking their strokes wherever a piece of gear would hide them. Keep all crossings and occlusions legible rather than tangling the contours.

This is a lively training product, not a technical diagram, so please render it with soft watercolor washes and loose contours while keeping the equipment recognizable. Include the product name “COURT CIRCLE” and a small “BALL-HANDLING KIT” line as part of the package design; let the ball and carefully woven orbit structure remain the visual focus.","skills":["layering","composition","constraint_following"]}]}
